course: dictionary
lesson: 8007
title: Redundant: not needed any more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Redundant» means not needed any more. A word is redundant when it repeats an idea that is already there, or when there is already enough of something.
explain: In writing, «free gift» is redundant, because a gift is already free. Take out the extra word and the sentence is just as clear, and often clearer.
explain: In maths, redundant information is extra information you do not need to solve the problem. Spot it, ignore it, and keep the facts you actually need.
explain: Grown-ups use «made redundant» in a gentle way to say someone has lost their job because the work is no longer needed. It is not anyone's fault. Use the context to tell which meaning is used.
fact: Redundant means not needed any more, because there is already enough or it is repeated.
fact: In writing, a redundant word repeats an idea: «free gift» says free twice, because a gift is already free.
fact: In maths, redundant information is extra information that you do not need to solve the problem.
fact: «Made redundant» (UK, Ireland and Australia) means losing your job because the employer no longer needs the work done.
fact: «Made redundant» means losing a job because there is no work for it; it is not anyone's fault, and families can get through a move together.
exercise: Which word repeats an idea already in «a free gift»? | a | free | gift | *Both are needed
exercise: A maths problem gives the shop's name and the price of 3 apples. The shop's name is not needed to find the cost. What does redundant mean here? | Written in red ink | Very important, so you must use it | Repeated on every line | *Not needed to solve the problem
exercise: «Dad was made redundant last month.» What does this mean? | *He lost his job because the work was no longer needed | He got a big pay rise | He moved to a new town | He went on holiday
test: Which phrase has a redundant word? | a new discovery | *past history | the history of Rome
test: «Made redundant» means losing your job. What does redundant mean in that phrase? | Very angry | Lost in the snow | *No longer needed, so the job was cut | Full of energy
exam: Which sentence explains redundant best? | Redundant means very old | Redundant means very wet and grey | *Redundant means not needed any more, because there is already enough or it is repeated | Redundant means shiny and new
